<DocPage
	eyebrow="Install"
	title="Get HippOS running"
	description="Flash the image to a USB drive, boot it, and play. You can install to an internal disk from the menu once you're happy with it."
>
	<h2>What you need</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>A 64-bit x86 PC or handheld that boots in UEFI mode and meets the <a href="/hardware#minimum-specs">minimum specs</a>.</li>
		<li>A USB drive or external SSD of <strong>32 GB or more</strong>. The image is about 4.7 GB to download and 19 GB once written.</li>
		<li>Membership of the HippOS Discord with the Early Access role. HippOS is in early access, and downloads are linked to Discord.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>1. Download</h2>
	<p>
		Sign in with Discord on the <a href="/">home page</a> and grab <code>hippos-amd64.img.zst</code>.
		The link always points at the latest beta.
	</p>

	<h2>2. Write it to a drive</h2>
	<p>Writing the image erases everything on the target drive.</p>
	<h3>Linux</h3>
	<p>
		Use <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/software/">Raspberry Pi Imager</a> (version 2.0.11 or newer). Under
		<strong>Operating System</strong> choose <strong>Use custom</strong> and pick <code>hippos-amd64.img.zst</code>
		as downloaded. There's no need to extract it first. Then choose your USB drive and write.
	</p>
	<h3>Windows</h3>
	<p>
		Use <a href="https://rufus.ie/">Rufus</a> (version 4.7 or newer). Select your USB drive, click
		<strong>Select</strong> and pick <code>hippos-amd64.img.zst</code> as downloaded, then press <strong>Start</strong>.
		If Rufus asks how to write the image, choose <strong>DD Image mode</strong>.
	</p>

	<h2>3. Boot</h2>
	<p>
		Plug the drive in, open your machine's boot menu and choose the USB drive. On the first boot HippOS expands
		its data partition to fill the drive and sets itself up. This takes a little longer than usual. Then you land
		in EmulationStation.
	</p>
	<p>
		If you only see a black screen or a frozen splash, try another display output first. If that doesn't help, ask
		in the Discord and include your logs (see the <a href="/faq">FAQ</a>).
	</p>

	<h2>4. Install to an internal disk (optional)</h2>
	<p>
		HippOS runs fine from USB. To move it onto an internal drive, open
		<strong>Main Menu → System Settings → Storage → Install on a new disk</strong>. You can wipe a whole disk,
		install into an existing partition, or use free space next to another OS. Each option needs at least 16 GB.
	</p>

	<h2>5. Add your games</h2>
	<ul>
		<li><strong>Over the network:</strong> HippOS shares its data folder over Samba. Open <code>\\HIPPOS\userdata</code> (Windows) or <code>smb://hippos/userdata</code> (Linux/macOS) and copy games into the matching folder under <code>roms</code>.</li>
		<li><strong>External drives:</strong> plug in a drive. HippOS merges its ROMs, BIOS and saves folders into your library automatically.</li>
		<li><strong>BIOS files:</strong> some systems need BIOS files you supply yourself. Put them in <code>userdata/bios</code>.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Updating</h2>
	<p>
		Installed systems update over the air from <strong>Main Menu → Updates &amp; Downloads</strong>. Updates replace
		the OS only. Your games, saves and settings stay on their own partition. See what changed in the
		<a href="/changelog">changelog</a>.
	</p>

	<p>More detailed guides live on the <a href={WIKI_URL}>HippOS wiki</a>.</p>
</DocPage>
